    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Asthma exacerbation:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " give for all but the mildest (not needed if PEF normalizes after 1–2 bronchodilator doses); early use often aborts the exacerbation, ↓ hospitalization and relapse. PO and IV are equally effective."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Adults:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " prednisone 40 mg once daily for milder outpatient exacerbations, 50–60 mg once daily for inpatient; usually 5–7 days in adults (3–5 in children). Prednisone 40–60 mg/day for 3–10 days per MSD's dose table. IV methylprednisolone if a line is in place, switch to PO when convenient. No taper needed if also on an ICS."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "Contraindicated in systemic fungal infection. No live vaccines at immunosuppressive doses. Caution: HF, hypertension, renal insufficiency; peptic ulcer, diverticulitis (perforation risk)."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "CYP3A4 inducers (phenytoin, carbamazepine, rifampin, barbiturates) may require a higher steroid dose; inhibitors (ketoconazole, itraconazole, ritonavir, erythromycin) raise levels. NSAIDs/aspirin: ↑ GI adverse effects. Warfarin: response usually inhibited; monitor INR often. K-depleting diuretics: watch for hypokalemia."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Watch for infection. Long-term:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " consider osteoporosis prevention (calcium, vitamin D, bisphosphonate, weight-bearing exercise); use the lowest effective dose and duration."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "Don't stop abruptly; taper after prolonged use (adrenal insufficiency risk for up to 12 months). Tell other clinicians you take steroids; seek care promptly for fever or infection."
     }
    ]
   }
  ]
 },
